# Design: Tower-state auto-save slice (T085)

## Problem
Section 18 needs an auto-save, but only the tower of T025 exposed a
save contract. No writer, reader, or slot existed. The task carve-out
(T085) ships the slice: the save holds tower state, and the save point
sits at the tower switch.

## Approach
Three small classes under game/scripts/save: SaveData (versioned
document, tower phase per city id), SaveStore (one JSON slot under
user://saves/slot.json; corrupt or foreign slots read as no save),
SaveSystem (records the switch, answers the load path). CityRun
composes a SaveSystem per run instead of adding an autoload:
cross-scene persistence comes from the file, not a live node, and the
CLAUDE.md rule says to keep autoloads minimal.

## Key Trade-offs
- Composition over autoload: T040 can still promote this to an
  autoload later; every save point it needs (cafe entry, boss cured,
  death) already lives inside CityRun.
- save_system stays null on a headless build until a test injects one
  (the DisplayServer precedent of AddressAudioDirector). Without the
  gate, existing city-run suites would read and write one shared
  user:// slot and contaminate each other across runs.
- The load path restores only the tower beat: tower phase, silenced
  poles, cut address, progress, quest chain. No wake-up is granted on
  load (one per switch, ever), and the intro tutorial skips the load
  so a leftover slot cannot break the fuse step. Restoring the rest
  (position, meter, inventory, and so on) belongs to T040.
- The instance JSON.parse replaces JSON.parse_string in SaveStore
  because the static call prints an engine ERROR on a corrupt slot and
  the project demands pristine test output.

